From: OSv projmax agent
Subject: [PATCH] zfs: prove the vdev_nonrot lever at runtime (NONROTPROOF/VDEVIO)

The standing rule on this campaign is that an A/B is valid only if the lever's
RESOLVED value is printed to the console and read back per arm, and that source
presence is not proof a fix is live.  Patch 0044 sets vd->vdev_nonrot, but
nothing observable changes at boot, so a build could carry it and still be
measured as though it did not.

Two proof lines, both printf (NOT debugf, which is verbose-gated and silent by
default -- that has already cost this campaign a run):

  NONROTPROOF, once per vdev from vdev_disk_open(): the resolved vdev_nonrot,
  the compile-time default behind it, and the 128 KiB cap the flag selects.
  This proves the FLAG.

  VDEVIO, every 10 s from the bio submit path in vdev_disk_io_start(): bio
  count, total bytes, the count of requests longer than SPA_OLD_MAXBLOCKSIZE
  (128 KiB), and the longest request seen.  This proves the flag is HONOURED:
  with vdev_nonrot correct, vdev_queue_aggregate() cannot build a span above
  the non-rotating cap, so gt128k must stay 0.  A nonzero gt128k alongside
  nonrot=1 is exactly the failure a source grep cannot detect.

Total bytes is reported as an absolute quantity, not normalised per
transaction: a kB-per-transaction ratio previously produced a bogus 4.8x on
this campaign because write volume is checkpoint/WAL/autovacuum backlog and
not proportional to throughput.

